{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-274841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order**, the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-274841" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={120}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 240=28\\,800.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,300"] ["math" "18\\,500"] ["math" "23\\,400"] ["math" "28\\,800"] ["math" "9\\,700"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "28\\,800"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14\\,175"] ["math" "28\\,800"] ["math" "31\\,500"] ["math" "32\\,400"] ["math" "34\\,000"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-28993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14\\,175"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,150"] ["math" "28\\,800"] ["math" "31\\,500"] ["math" "34\\,000"] ["math" "9\\,900"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-76488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "31\\,500"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6.2"] ["math" "7.3"] ["math" "8.6"] ["math" "8.7"] ["math" "9.2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8.6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{3}{4})≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324392" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324401" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2.3})≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "115.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-324397" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "244.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4)=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4) & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-16 & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=86.416\\,678{…}^{∘}≈{86.417}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.417}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/15e99d348206191535a2b6fb9d85893e666abd16f5f9fc61fdf4e19424e2fde9.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{86.417}^{∘} \\\\  & ={273.583}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=86.417\\cdot 273.583≈23\\,642,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,894"] ["math" "19\\,876"] ["math" "23\\,642"] ["math" "27\\,128"] ["math" "7\\,653"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-30468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "23\\,642"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},-{60}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=60\\cdot (-60)=-3\\,600.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29177" "field" nil "attribute" 