thin the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-29179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ec93222686d7127d6a0b0f230de357b7dd6a9281db371524cde917072b9cb1b"} {"question" "q-29179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850