" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "q-120481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/state.json" "file_sha256" "ec26c54c8f04ebce6ed3bc4053aaf832400adca120bb72ae9828c01eaaaa7850"} {"question" "e-3527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x <             2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3527.json" "file_sha256" "edba9343e2385768368aa03f81c4d2488bcc3172a09d23d6d64001fd2018c796"} {"question" "e-3527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/0e370925faa2a4abc7ac613657ecdf27c2fa0eaa66cff05b077f77a71999c742.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3527.json" "file_sha256" "edba9343e2385768368aa03f81c4d2488bcc3172a09d23d6d64001fd2018c796"} {"question" "e-4260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-4260.json" "file_sha256" "856f4e5756050b481df2ab67fdc6517edcc8b5d8fb06e87ee0e3e9c31c9c4e87"} {"question" "e-4260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-4260.json" "file_sha256" "856f4e5756050b481df2ab67fdc6517edcc8b5d8fb06e87ee0e3e9c31c9c4e87"} {"question" "e-3529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq  x <            180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3529.json" "file_sha256" "717189aeb6b2f939668163927f75c2e5f89ba756a4333cbbc341316aade6c760"} {"question" "e-3529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 45^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3529.json" "file_sha256" "717189aeb6b2f939668163927f75c2e5f89ba756a4333cbbc341316aade6c760"} {"question" "e-3532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi?$\n\n- $\\sec x  = -1$\n\n- $\\sec x  = 2$\n\n- $\\sec x  = \\dfrac 1 3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3532.json" "file_sha256" "46ec3c22925b4939e21634383812ac202ba917dd104e5c46ea41576105478e99"} {"question" "e-3532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/example-3532.json" "file_sha256" "46ec3c22925b4939e21634383812ac202ba917dd104e5c46ea41576105478e99"}] "usage" {"e-4260" [] "q-29177" [] "q-29179" [] "e-3532" [] "e-3529" [] "q-29163" [] "e-3527" []} "basis" 808 "retractions" [[17592186115625 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x = 2$ for $-180^{∘} \\le x < 180^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"] [17592186115626 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x = 2$ for $-360^{∘} \\le x < 0^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"] [17592186115624 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = 1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{2}$"] [17592186114922 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?"] [17592186114922 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sec x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sec x &= -4 \\\\\n\\sec x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/edfa3375bc3db6b46d1dac36152c42cd.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}\n$$"] [17592186114967 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?"] [17592186114967 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{3}) = 1.230959… \\approx 1.2310\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/9ea131571a45a21b7339dc32090c32f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - 1.2310 \\\\\n&= 5.0522\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186114923 :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq x < 180^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?"] [17592186114923 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) = 45^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/1b44825deb488f131a714655b48634f9.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025\n$$"] [17592186114924 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = -1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\dfrac 1 3$"] [17592186114924 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$[MATH: \\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).]$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113145 17592186113146 1759218611